\title{Reflective Type Theory: A Semantic Core for Propositions About Their Own Provability}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a semantic core for a \emph{reflective} type theory --- a system in which propositions may speak about their own provability. Adopting the propositions-as-types discipline in its possible-worlds form, we interpret a proposition as the set of \emph{proof stages} (worlds) at which it holds, and we attach a provability modality $\Box$ (``is provable'') to an accessibility relation $R$ encoding a single step of reasoning. We establish three headline results. First, the self-referential proposition $\Box P \wedge \neg\,\Box\Box P$ --- ``provable but not provably provable'' --- is inhabited: we exhibit an explicit finite model realizing it, and prove the matching boundary theorem that on any \emph{transitive} frame the axiom $\Box P \to \Box\Box P$ holds, so the phenomenon is a genuine feature of non-transitive provability rather than of classical G\"odel--L\"ob provability. Second, the modality is \emph{normal} --- monotone, distributive over conjunction, validating the distribution axiom $K$ and necessitation --- yet provably distinct from the identity operator, so the reflective theory properly extends its non-modal base. Third, viewing propositions as a complete lattice, $\Box$ is one monotone operator among all monotone operators; the Knaster--Tarski theorem supplies least and greatest fixed points uniformly, the modal duality $\Diamond P = \comp{\Box\comp{P}}$ holds, and on well-founded frames L\"ob's law $\Box(\Box P \to P) \to \Box P$ is validated. These facts identify the reflective proof-term language, structurally, as a modal $\mu$-calculus.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Self-reference is simultaneously the source of logic's most famous limitations --- the incompleteness phenomena of G\"odel and the fixed-point theorem of L\"ob --- and of some of its most powerful tools, the fixed-point logics that drive formal verification. A \emph{reflective} type theory is one whose propositions are permitted to comment on their own provability: alongside statements like ``$P$ holds'' we admit statements like ``$P$ is provable'' and ``$P$ is provable but its provability is not provable.''

The immediate difficulty is that ``provable'' cannot be an absolute, self-applied predicate without inviting paradox. Our resolution is to relativize provability to \emph{stages of knowledge}: a proposition is the set of stages at which it holds, and provability at a stage is a promise about the stages reachable by one step of reasoning. This is the possible-worlds reading of a necessity modality, keeping the development elementary and finite-model-checkable.

Three questions organize the paper: \emph{expressibility and inhabitation} of ``provable but not provably provable'' (Section~\ref{sec:pnpp}); how the reflective layer \emph{extends} the non-modal base (Section~\ref{sec:extension}); and the algebraic \emph{structure} of the reflective connectives (Section~\ref{sec:mu}). The answers are: yes, exactly on non-transitive frames; a proper (normal, non-trivial) extension; and a modal $\mu$-calculus, with L\"ob's law as its well-founded fixed-point principle.

\section{The reflective semantics}

\begin{definition}[Reflective frame]
A \emph{reflective frame} on a type of worlds $\W$ is a binary accessibility relation $R : \W \to \W \to \mathrm{Prop}$, with $R\,w\,v$ read as ``from stage $w$, stage $v$ is one provability step ahead.'' A \emph{proposition} is a subset $P \subseteq \W$, understood as the set of stages at which it holds. Propositions ordered by $\subseteq$ form a complete Boolean lattice with conjunction $P \cap Q$, disjunction $P \cup Q$, negation $\comp P = \W \setminus P$, top $\W$, and bottom $\varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reflective modalities]
For a frame $(\W, R)$ and $P \subseteq \W$,
\[
\Box P = \{\, w \in \W : \forall v,\ R\,w\,v \Rightarrow v \in P \,\}, \qquad
\Diamond P = \{\, w \in \W : \exists v,\ R\,w\,v \wedge v \in P \,\}.
\]
We read $w \in \Box P$ as ``$P$ is provable at stage $w$'' and $w \in \Diamond P$ as ``$P$ is consistent with provability at $w$.''
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Modal duality]
For every frame and every $P$, $\ \Diamond P = \comp{\Box\comp{P}}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$w \notin \Box\comp P$ means not every $R$-successor of $w$ lies in $\comp P$, i.e.\ some successor lies in $P$, i.e.\ $w \in \Diamond P$. Taking complements gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\section{Provable but not provably provable}\label{sec:pnpp}

\begin{definition}[The chain frame]
Let $\W = \{0,1,2\}$ and let $R$ be the non-transitive chain $2 \to 1 \to 0$, i.e.\ $R\,a\,b$ iff $(a=2 \wedge b=1)$ or $(a=1 \wedge b=0)$. Let $P = \{1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Inhabitation]\label{thm:pnpp}
On the chain frame, $2 \in \Box P$ and $2 \notin \Box\Box P$. Equivalently, the type $\Box P \wedge \neg\,\Box\Box P$ is inhabited.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The unique successor of $2$ is $1 \in P$, so $2 \in \Box P$. Suppose $2 \in \Box\Box P$; then $\Box P$ holds at the successor $1$, so $1 \in \Box P$. But the unique successor of $1$ is $0 \notin P$, so $1 \notin \Box P$, a contradiction. Hence $2 \notin \Box\Box P$.
\end{proof}

A direct finite computation confirms $\Box P = \{0,2\}$ and $\Box\Box P = \{0,1\}$.

\begin{corollary}
There exist a frame $F$, a proposition $P$, and a world $w$ with $w \in \Box P$ and $w \notin \Box\Box P$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Transitivity validates axiom 4]\label{thm:four}
If the frame is transitive ($R\,a\,b$ and $R\,b\,c$ imply $R\,a\,c$), then $\Box P \subseteq \Box\Box P$ for every $P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $w \in \Box P$, and take $v, u$ with $R\,w\,v$ and $R\,v\,u$. By transitivity $R\,w\,u$, so $u \in P$ since $w \in \Box P$. Thus $v \in \Box P$ for every successor $v$, i.e.\ $w \in \Box\Box P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:four} is the sharp boundary: Theorem~\ref{thm:pnpp} requires non-transitivity, which the chain frame has ($R\,2\,1$ and $R\,1\,0$ hold but $R\,2\,0$ fails). Adjoining $R\,2\,0$ restores axiom 4 and destroys the witness. The classical provability logics $K4$ and $GL$ are transitive, so the phenomenon is genuinely non-classical.
\end{remark}

\section{The reflective modality properly extends the base}\label{sec:extension}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
If $P \subseteq Q$ then $\Box P \subseteq \Box Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $w \in \Box P$ every successor lies in $P \subseteq Q$, so $w \in \Box Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Distribution over conjunction]
$\Box(P \cap Q) = \Box P \cap \Box Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every successor lies in $P \cap Q$ iff every successor lies in $P$ and every successor lies in $Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Axiom $K$]
For all $P,Q$ and every $w$: if $w \in \Box\{v : v \in P \Rightarrow v \in Q\}$ and $w \in \Box P$, then $w \in \Box Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For a successor $v$ of $w$, the hypotheses give $v \in P \Rightarrow v \in Q$ and $v \in P$, hence $v \in Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Necessitation]
If $P = \W$ then $\Box P = \W$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every successor of every world lies in $\W = P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\Box$ is not the identity]\label{thm:neid}
There is a frame on $\{0,1\}$, a proposition $P$, and a world $w$ with $w \in \Box P \not\Leftrightarrow w \in P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $R = \varnothing$ and $P = \varnothing$. Every world is vacuously in $\Box P$, so $\Box P = \W$, while $P = \varnothing$. At $w = 0$, $0 \in \Box P$ but $0 \notin P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Proper extension]
The reflective modality is normal (Theorems~\ref{thm:mono}--\ref{thm:neid}) yet not definable as the identity, so the reflective theory properly extends its non-modal fragment: it validates every structural law of provability while adding genuinely new propositions.
\end{corollary}

\section{The proof-term language is a modal $\mu$-calculus}\label{sec:mu}

\begin{proposition}
$\mathcal{P}(\W)$ ordered by $\subseteq$ is a complete lattice, and $\Box$, $\Diamond$, and every operator built from them with $\cap,\cup$ are monotone.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Knaster--Tarski]
Every monotone $f : \mathcal{P}(\W) \to \mathcal{P}(\W)$ has a least fixed point $\mu f = \bigcap\{X : f(X) \subseteq X\}$ and a greatest fixed point $\nu f = \bigcup\{X : X \subseteq f(X)\}$.
\end{theorem}

Since $\Box$ and $\Diamond$ are monotone, the fixed-point binders $\mu$ (least) and $\nu$ (greatest) are available uniformly, upgrading the modal logic to a modal $\mu$-calculus.

\begin{example}
$\mu X.\,(A \cup \Diamond X)$ is the set of stages from which $A$ is eventually reachable; $\nu X.\,\Diamond X$ is the set of stages beginning an infinite forward path. On $0 \to 1 \to 2 \to 3$ with $A=\{3\}$, the former is $\{0,1,2,3\}$ and the latter is $\varnothing$.
\end{example}

\begin{interpretation}
Under propositions-as-types, a proof term witnesses membership in the corresponding set of stages; the available constructors are exactly the monotone lattice operators together with $\mu$ and $\nu$. Hence the reflective proof-term language is, structurally, a modal $\mu$-calculus.
\end{interpretation}

\begin{theorem}[L\"ob's law]
If the frame is transitive and converse well-founded (no infinite chain $w_0 \to w_1 \to \cdots$), then $\Box(\Box P \to P) \subseteq \Box P$ for every $P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By well-founded induction on the step relation. Assume $w \in \Box(\Box P \to P)$ and let $v$ be a successor of $w$. By transitivity $v$ inherits $\Box(\Box P \to P)$, so the induction hypothesis at $v$ gives $v \in \Box P$; the hypothesis at $w$ then yields $\Box P \to P$ at $v$, hence $v \in P$. As $v$ was arbitrary, $w \in \Box P$.
\end{proof}

L\"ob's law says: if it is provable that ``the provability of $P$ entails $P$,'' then $P$ is already provable. It is the modal core of G\"odel's second incompleteness theorem and the $\mu$-calculus fixed-point law for the provability modality; well-foundedness makes the self-referential induction terminate.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Modal evaluation.} For a finite frame and $P \subseteq \W$, place each $w$ in $\Box P$ iff $R(w) \subseteq P$ and in $\Diamond P$ iff $R(w) \cap P \neq \varnothing$, where $R(w) = \{v : R\,w\,v\}$; complexity $O(|\W| + |R|)$. Iterating decides $\Box P \wedge \neg\,\Box\Box P$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Fixed-point iteration.} For monotone $f$, compute $\mu f$ by iterating from $\varnothing$ and $\nu f$ from $\W$ until stabilization (at most $|\W|$ steps), evaluating any closed $\mu$-calculus formula.

\section{Applications and future work}

The distinction between $\Box P$ and $\Box\Box P$ models what a reasoner can guarantee now versus about its own future guarantees; non-transitive frames capture bounded-horizon reasoning. The modal $\mu$-calculus identified above is the specification language of model checking, with the two algorithms forming its core. The framework is a controlled laboratory for self-reference, locating the safe/explosive boundary at transitivity plus well-foundedness.

We conjecture: (1) $\Box P \wedge \neg\,\Box\Box P$ is satisfiable iff the step relation is non-transitive, with axiom 4 equivalent to transitivity on definable predicates; (2) every $\mu$-calculus formula is realized by a reflective proof term and conversely, via an alternation-preserving translation; (3) strong normalization holds iff provability is transitive and converse well-founded, i.e.\ iff L\"ob's law holds globally; (4) grading the step relation yields a hierarchy $\Box_0 \subseteq \Box_1 \subseteq \cdots$ whose union is a fixed point.

\section{Conclusion}

Reflective type theory is normal modal logic over propositions-as-sets, properly above its non-modal base, with the modal $\mu$-calculus as its fixed-point completion and L\"ob's theorem as its well-founded fixed-point law. ``Provable but not provably provable'' is not a paradox but a theorem-bearing citizen of the theory, inhabited exactly at the horizon of non-transitive provability and vanishing once provability becomes far-sighted enough to see its own consequences.

\end{document}

